{"author":{"id":"1f592c0ac5a83ec8bdb9b8574bd8dddf2082ee3c98ad9608c7b89c82d05faf99"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792352321,"nonce":"Igakt2+b5PEMxuavg6SnQPyn750=","title":"A page's player cannot seek in a file from a server that does not answer range requests","message":"**What happens**\n\nThe page agent gives the audio element the file's URL. From a plain static server, one that answers a range request with the whole file and no `Accept-Ranges` (Ash's own `ash serve` is one, and sends `Cache-Control: no-store`), the element is not seekable: its `seekable` range is empty, and setting `currentTime` sends it back to 0. In the dashboard app the seek key and the scrubber went to the start of the track at every drag.\n\nChecked in Chrome against `ash serve`: `currentTime = 20` read back 0 on the element given the URL, and 20 on one given a blob of the same file.\n\n**What it does now**\n\nFor a file from the page's own origin the agent fetches it first. If the server answers by range, or the file is over 64 MB, the element streams it as before. Otherwise the whole file is read into memory and played from there, and the memory is let go when the player is closed. A cross-origin URL is left to the element. A play that is asked for before the file has come is made when the source is set, as a browser refuses a play of an element with no source.\n\nThe page agent test covers both paths.","files":null}]}